[2026-11-28T10:30:53.718Z] [INFO]       "output": "<file>\n00001| # Upload your full memory to a bug report\n00002| \n00003| When you report a bug from the [formal-ai demo](https://link-assistant.github.io/formal-ai),\n00004| the maintainers can almost always reproduce the issue **only** when they have the\n00005| full memory of the agent at the moment it went wrong. This page is the single\n00006| link the prefilled \"Report issue\" body points to, so the issue body itself can\n00007| stay short while still giving you everything you need to attach the export.\n00008| \n00009| ## What \"full memory\" means\n00010| \n00011| Clicking **Export memory** in the topbar writes `formal-ai-memory.lino`. The\n00012| file is a complete [`formal_ai_bundle`](case-studies/issue-18/README.md): the\n00013| entire seed (rules, concepts, tools, multilingual responses), your UI\n00014| preferences (demo mode, diagnostics mode), environment metadata (version, URL,\n00015| user agent), and the append-only event log of every turn, reasoning step, and\n00016| tool invocation in the session. With that file a maintainer can reconstitute\n00017| the exact session that triggered the bug — no extra steps required.\n00018| \n00019| ## Step 1 — Export it\n00020| \n00021| 1. Open [https://link-assistant.github.io/formal-ai](https://link-assistant.github.io/formal-ai).\n00022| 2. Reproduce the bug, or stop right after you saw it.\n00023| 3. Click **Export memory** in the topbar. The browser saves\n00024|    `formal-ai-memory.lino` to your downloads folder.\n00025| \n00026| The same export is available in the CLI:\n00027| \n00028| ```bash\n00029| cargo run --manifest-path rust/Cargo.toml -- memory export --from memory.lino --path formal-ai-memory.lino\n00030| ```\n00031| \n00032| ## Step 2 — Redact sensitive content\n00033| \n00034| The export contains everything you typed into the chat, plus the assistant's\n00035| replies and tool outputs. Before you share it, open `formal-ai-memory.lino` in\n00036| any text editor (it is plain UTF-8) and remove:\n00037| \n00038| - personal names, emails, phone numbers, addresses\n00039| - API keys, tokens, passwords, internal URLs\n00040| - any pasted source code or document you are not comfortable publishing\n00041| \n00042| If you are unsure whether something is safe to share, redact it. The bundle\n00043| remains useful even with redacted strings replaced by `[REDACTED]`.\n00044| \n00045| ## Step 3 — Attach the file (choose one)\n00046| \n00047| GitHub's issue uploader does not currently include `.lino` in its\n00048| [supported attachment types](https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/writing-on-github/working-with-advanced-formatting/attaching-files),\n00049| so you cannot drag `formal-ai-memory.lino` directly into the issue body. Two\n00050| workarounds both work; pick whichever is easier for you.\n00051| \n00052| ### Option A — Upload as a GitHub Gist (no zipping needed)\n00053| \n00054| GitHub Gists accept arbitrary file extensions, so this is the lightest path.\n00055| \n00056| 1. Open [https://gist.github.com](https://gist.github.com).\n00057| 2. In the filename field type `formal-ai-memory.lino`.\n00058| 3. Paste the file contents (or drag the file in).\n00059| 4. Choose **Create secret gist** if you want a private link, or **Create\n00060|    public gist** if the content is safe to publish.\n00061| 5. Copy the gist URL and paste it into the issue body.\n00062| \n00063| Secret gists are not indexed and only people with the link can view them, but\n00064| they are still readable to anyone with the URL — redact first.\n00065| \n00066| ### Option B — Wrap the file in a `.zip` and attach it\n00067| \n00068| GitHub does accept `.zip` attachments on issues. Wrap the file locally:\n00069| \n00070| - **macOS**: right-click `formal-ai-memory.lino` → *Compress* → drag the\n00071|   resulting `formal-ai-memory.zip` into the issue body.\n00072| - **Windows**: right-click `formal-ai-memory.lino` → *Send to* → *Compressed\n00073|   (zipped) folder* → drag the resulting `formal-ai-memory.zip` into the issue\n00074|   body.\n00075| - **Linux / WSL / macOS terminal**:\n00076|   ```bash\n00077|   zip formal-ai-memory.zip formal-ai-memory.lino\n00078|   ```\n00079|   Then drag the resulting `.zip` into the issue body.\n00080| \n00081| ## Re-importing a bundle\n00082| \n00083| A maintainer (or you, on a different machine) can reconstruct the session with:\n00084| \n00085| 1. Click **Import memory** in the topbar, then pick the `.lino` file.\n00086| 2. Or from the CLI:\n00087|    ```bash\n00088|    cargo run --manifest-path rust/Cargo.toml -- memory import --path formal-ai-memory.lino --into memory.lino\n00089|    ```\n00090| \n00091| Both surfaces auto-detect the bundle vs. the legacy `demo_memory` format and\n00092| report any seed-version migrations they suggest.\n00093| \n00094| ## Why a `.lino` upload is not yet native\n00095| \n00096| GitHub maintains a server-side allow-list of file extensions for issue and PR\n00097| attachments. `.lino` is not on that list at the time of writing, so the\n00098| uploader silently rejects it. We file this as an upstream limitation rather\n00099| than a formal-ai bug; the `.zip` and Gist workarounds above are the\n00100| documented escape hatch until GitHub broadens the allow-list.\n\n(End of file - total 100 lines)\n</file>",
